🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗛𝗮𝘀 𝗡𝗼 𝗘𝗻𝗱𝗽𝗼𝗶𝗻𝘁𝘀 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes Service exists, but kubectl get endpoints shows no endpoints. How will you troubleshoot it?”

This is a common issue where the Service exists, but it isn't finding the expected pods.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check the Service

kubectl get svc <service-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Service configuration.

2️⃣ Check the Service selector

kubectl describe svc <service-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

Selector: app=myapp

3️⃣ Check pod labels

kubectl get pods --show-labels -n <namespace>

Compare the pod labels with the Service selector.

For example:

Service selector: app=myapp
Pod label:        app=backend

The Service won't select that pod.

4️⃣ Check whether pods are Ready

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

A pod may exist but still not be considered a usable endpoint if it isn't Ready.

Check:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

5️⃣ Check EndpointSlices

kubectl get endpointslices -n <namespace>

This can provide additional information about the endpoints associated with the Service.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Service → Selector → Pod Labels → Pod Readiness → EndpointSlices → Root Cause

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Service existing doesn't mean it has healthy endpoints.

When there are no endpoints, one of the first things I check is:

👉 Does the Service selector actually match the pod labels?
